WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:05.040
You've learned about
self-attention.

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.580
You've learned about
multi-headed attention.

3
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.240
Let's put it all together to

4
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.080
build the transformer network.

5
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.160
In this video,
you'll see how you

6
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.440
can pair the attention
mechanisms you

7
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.960
saw in the previous videos

8
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.140
to build the transformer
architecture.

9
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.820
Starting again with
the sentence 'Jane

10
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.345
visite L'Afrique en septembre'

11
00:00:26.345 --> 00:00:28.200
and its corresponding embedding.

12
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.120
Let's walk through how you can

13
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.600
translate the sentence
from French to English.

14
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.080
I've also added the start of

15
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.805
sentence and end of
sentence tokens here.

16
00:00:39.805 --> 00:00:42.630
Up until this point, for
the sake of simplicity,

17
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.230
I've only been talking about

18
00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.970
the embeddings for the
words in the sentence,

19
00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:51.170
but in many sequence-to-sequence
translation tasks,

20
00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.370
it will be useful to also add

21
00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.430
the start of sentence
or the SOS and

22
00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:57.990
the end of sentence or

23
00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:02.305
the EOS tokens which I
have in this example.

24
00:01:02.305 --> 00:01:05.070
The first step in
the transformer is,

25
00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:08.050
these embeddings get fed into

26
00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.960
an encoder block which has a
multi-head attention layer.

27
00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:16.830
This is exactly what you
saw on the last slide,

28
00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:21.580
where you feed in the values Q,

29
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.090
K and V computed from

30
00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.570
the embeddings and the weight
matrices W. This layer then

31
00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:33.310
produces a matrix that
can be passed into

32
00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:36.410
a feed-forward neural
network which helps

33
00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.490
determine what
interesting features

34
00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.030
there are in the sentence.

35
00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.730
In the transformer paper,

36
00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:46.860
this encoding block
is repeated n

37
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:52.960
times and a typical
value for n is six.

38
00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:57.890
After maybe about six
times through this block,

39
00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:00.140
we will then feed

40
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.480
the output of the encoder
into a decoder block.

41
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.735
Let's start building
the decoder block.

42
00:02:06.735 --> 00:02:09.400
The decoders block's job

43
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.020
is to output the
English translation.

44
00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.680
The first output will be the
start of sentence token,

45
00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:18.120
which I've already
written down here.

46
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.955
At every step, the
decoder block will

47
00:02:20.955 --> 00:02:24.420
input the first few words,

48
00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.820
whatever we've already
generated of the translation.

49
00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.820
When we're just getting started,

50
00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.080
the only thing we know is that

51
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.580
the translation will start with

52
00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.050
a start of sentence token.

53
00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:41.120
The start of sentence
token gets fed in to

54
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.505
this multi-head attention
block and just this one token,

55
00:02:45.505 --> 00:02:47.690
the SOS token,
start of sentence,

56
00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.500
is used to compute Q,

57
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.895
K and V for this multi-head
attention block.

58
00:02:53.895 --> 00:02:58.360
This first block's
output is used to

59
00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.520
generate the Q matrix

60
00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.995
for the next multi-head
attention block

61
00:03:04.995 --> 00:03:09.525
and the output of the
encoder is used to generate

62
00:03:09.525 --> 00:03:13.890
K and V. Here's

63
00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:17.430
a second multi-head attention
block with inputs Q,

64
00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.135
K and V as before.

65
00:03:20.135 --> 00:03:22.060
Why is it structured this way?

66
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.320
Maybe here's one piece of
intuition that could help.

67
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.060
The input down here is

68
00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:31.150
what you've translated
of the sentence so far.

69
00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.750
This will ask a query to say,

70
00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:36.115
"What of the start
of sentence?".

71
00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:39.180
It will then pull
context from K and V,

72
00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.560
which is translated from

73
00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.780
the French version
of the sentence to

74
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.220
then try to decide what is

75
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.900
the next word in the
sequence to generate.

76
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.120
To finish the description
of the decoded block,

77
00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.240
the multi-head attention block

78
00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.400
outputs the values which

79
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.620
are fed to a feed
forward neural network.

80
00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.900
This decoder block is also

81
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.875
going to be repeated n
times, maybe six times,

82
00:04:01.875 --> 00:04:04.380
where you take the output,
feed it back to the input,

83
00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.380
and have this go through,
say, half a dozen times.

84
00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.000
The job of this
neural network is

85
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.760
to predict the next
word in the sentence.

86
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.620
Hopefully, it will decide
that the first word

87
00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.860
in the English
translation is Jane.

88
00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:26.500
What we do is then feed
Jane to the input as well.

89
00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:31.760
Now, the next query comes from
SOS and Jane and it says,

90
00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.480
well, given Jane, what is the
most appropriate next word?

91
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.770
Let's find the right key
and the right value,

92
00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.940
then lets us generate the
most appropriate next word,

93
00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.645
which hopefully will
generate visite.

94
00:04:46.645 --> 00:04:51.270
Then running this neural
network again generates Africa.

95
00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:55.395
Then we feed Africa
back into the input.

96
00:04:55.395 --> 00:05:00.420
Hopefully it then generates
in and then September,

97
00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:01.980
and with this input,

98
00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.750
hopefully it
generates the end of

99
00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.235
sentence token and
then we're done.

100
00:05:06.235 --> 00:05:08.330
These encoder and
decoder blocks,

101
00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:09.650
and how they're combined

102
00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:11.660
to perform a
sequence to sequence

103
00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.880
translation tasks
are the main ideas

104
00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:15.865
behind the transformer
architecture.

105
00:05:15.865 --> 00:05:18.360
In this case, you
saw how you can

106
00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.670
translate an input sentence into

107
00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.920
a sentence in another language

108
00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.830
to gain some intuition
about how attention

109
00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:28.185
in neural networks can be combined

110
00:05:28.185 --> 00:05:31.350
to allow simultaneous
computation.

111
00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.165
But beyond these main ideas,

112
00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:36.525
there are a few extra bells
and whistles to transformers.

113
00:05:36.525 --> 00:05:39.570
Let me briefly step through
these extra bells and

114
00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.085
whistles that makes

115
00:05:41.085 --> 00:05:44.070
the transformer network
work even better.

116
00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:49.335
The first of these is positional
encoding of the input.

117
00:05:49.335 --> 00:05:52.845
If you recall the self
attention equations,

118
00:05:52.845 --> 00:05:56.205
there's nothing that indicates
the position of a word.

119
00:05:56.205 --> 00:05:59.775
Is this word the first
word in the sentence,

120
00:05:59.775 --> 00:06:02.220
in the middle, the last
word in the sentence?

121
00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:04.560
But the position within
a sentence can be

122
00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.095
extremely important
to translation.

123
00:06:07.095 --> 00:06:08.820
The way you encode

124
00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.940
the position of elements
in the input is that you

125
00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:17.370
use a combination of these
sine and cosine equations.

126
00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.185
Let's say, for example,

127
00:06:19.185 --> 00:06:24.330
that your word embedding is
a vector with four values.

128
00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:28.260
In this case, the dimension D of

129
00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:33.650
the word embedding is 4,

130
00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:36.780
so x^(1), x^(2),

131
00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.065
x^(3), let's say those are
four dimensional vectors.

132
00:06:40.065 --> 00:06:43.335
In this example, we're
going to then create

133
00:06:43.335 --> 00:06:45.780
a positional embedding vector of

134
00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.960
the same dimension,
also four dimensional.

135
00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.795
I'm going to call this
positional embedding p^(1),

136
00:06:54.795 --> 00:06:57.540
let's say for the
position embedding

137
00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.970
of the first word Jane.

138
00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:03.480
In this equation below, pos,

139
00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:07.815
position denotes the numerical
position of the word.

140
00:07:07.815 --> 00:07:09.945
For the word Jane,

141
00:07:09.945 --> 00:07:15.180
pos = 1,

142
00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.300
and i over here refers to

143
00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.855
the different
dimensions of encoding.

144
00:07:21.855 --> 00:07:26.145
This first element
corresponds to i = 0.

145
00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:28.960
This element i = 0,

146
00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:32.215
i =1, i = 1.

147
00:07:32.215 --> 00:07:36.380
These are the
variables pos and i,

148
00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:40.115
they go into these
equations down below.

149
00:07:40.115 --> 00:07:42.925
Where pos is the
position of a word,

150
00:07:42.925 --> 00:07:46.545
i goes from 0 to 1,

151
00:07:46.545 --> 00:07:49.230
and d = 4,

152
00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.375
is the dimension of this vector.

153
00:07:51.375 --> 00:07:55.080
What the position encoding
does with the sine and

154
00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:59.595
cosine is create a unique
positional encoding vector.

155
00:07:59.595 --> 00:08:03.600
One of these vectors that
is unique for each word,

156
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:10.155
the vector p^(3) that encodes
the position of l'Afrique,

157
00:08:10.155 --> 00:08:14.310
the third word will be a set
of four values that'll be

158
00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:16.980
different than the
four values used in

159
00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.070
code position of the
first word of Jane.

160
00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:25.065
This is what the sine and
cosine occurs look like.

161
00:08:25.065 --> 00:08:27.345
Is i = 0,

162
00:08:27.345 --> 00:08:28.650
i = 0,

163
00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:32.355
i = 1, i =1.

164
00:08:32.355 --> 00:08:35.250
Because you have these terms and

165
00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:38.880
denominator you end up with i =

166
00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:47.565
0 will have some sinusoid
curve that looks like this,

167
00:08:47.565 --> 00:08:52.600
and i =0 will be
the matched cosine.

168
00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:57.900
90 degrees out of face and i

169
00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:04.660
=1 will end up with a
lower frequency sinusoid,

170
00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:13.660
and i =1 gives you a
matched cosine curve.

171
00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:18.120
For T1, for position 1,

172
00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.760
you read off values at

173
00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:25.650
this position to fill in
those four values there.

174
00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.320
Whereas for a different word
at a different position,

175
00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.605
maybe this is now three
on the horizontal axis,

176
00:09:31.605 --> 00:09:34.050
you read off a different
set of values.

177
00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.390
Notice these first two
values may be very

178
00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:38.955
similar because they're
roughly at the same height.

179
00:09:38.955 --> 00:09:42.630
But by using these multiple
sines and cosines,

180
00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.030
looking across all four values,

181
00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:49.270
P3 will be a different
vector than P1.

182
00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:54.060
The positional
encoding, P1j is added

183
00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:58.110
directly to X1 to the input this

184
00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:02.205
way so that each of the
word vectors is also

185
00:10:02.205 --> 00:10:04.290
influenced or colored with

186
00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:07.920
where in the sentence
the word appears.

187
00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:11.070
The output of the
encoding block contains

188
00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:14.175
contextual semantic embedding

189
00:10:14.175 --> 00:10:16.620
and positional
encoding information.

190
00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:20.100
The output of the
embedding layer is then D,

191
00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:22.905
which in this case four

192
00:10:22.905 --> 00:10:27.180
by the maximum length of
sequence, your model can take.

193
00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:32.740
The outputs of all these
layers are also of this shape.

194
00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:37.890
In addition to adding

195
00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:41.535
these position encodings
to the embeddings,

196
00:10:41.535 --> 00:10:43.140
you'd also pass them through

197
00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.170
the network with
residual connections.

198
00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.450
These residual connections
are similar to

199
00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.375
those you previously
see in the resnet.

200
00:10:51.375 --> 00:10:54.030
Their purpose in this
case is to pass along

201
00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:57.780
positional information through
the entire architecture.

202
00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.495
In addition to
positional encoding,

203
00:11:00.495 --> 00:11:02.970
the transformer
network also uses

204
00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:06.585
a layer very similar
to a batch norm.

205
00:11:06.585 --> 00:11:09.600
Their purpose in this
case is to pass along

206
00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:12.600
positional information
into position encoding.

207
00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:16.680
The transformer also uses
a layer add norm that is

208
00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.885
very similar to the
batch norm layer

209
00:11:18.885 --> 00:11:20.805
that you're already
familiar with.

210
00:11:20.805 --> 00:11:22.275
For the purpose of this video,

211
00:11:22.275 --> 00:11:23.595
don't worry about
the differences.

212
00:11:23.595 --> 00:11:25.260
Think of it as
playing a role very

213
00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.270
similar to the batch norm.

214
00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.490
This helps speed up learning

215
00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:32.355
and this batch norm like layer,

216
00:11:32.355 --> 00:11:34.140
this add & norm layer is

217
00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:37.275
repeated throughout
this architecture.

218
00:11:37.275 --> 00:11:41.250
Finally, for the output
of the decoder block,

219
00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:43.350
there's actually
also a linear and

220
00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:45.300
then a soft max layer to

221
00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:48.480
predict the next word
one word at a time.

222
00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:50.670
In case you read the literature

223
00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:52.785
on the transformer network,

224
00:11:52.785 --> 00:11:55.095
you may also hear
something called

225
00:11:55.095 --> 00:11:58.140
the mask multi-head attention,

226
00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:00.390
which I'm going to
draw and over here.

227
00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:02.775
Mask multi-head attention is

228
00:12:02.775 --> 00:12:05.670
important only during
the training process

229
00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:08.895
where you're using a
dataset of correct

230
00:12:08.895 --> 00:12:12.480
French to English translations
to train your transformer.

231
00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:14.940
Previously, we
stepped through how

232
00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:18.495
the transformer performs
prediction one word at the time,

233
00:12:18.495 --> 00:12:20.625
but how does it train?

234
00:12:20.625 --> 00:12:22.140
Let's say your dataset has a

235
00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:25.035
correct French to
English translation.

236
00:12:25.035 --> 00:12:27.165
Jane visite l'Afrique on September,

237
00:12:27.165 --> 00:12:29.910
and Jane visits
Africa in September.

238
00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:31.680
When training, you
have access to

239
00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.740
the entire correct
English translation,

240
00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:36.540
the correct output,

241
00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:39.810
and the correct input

242
00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:43.455
and because you have the
full correct output,

243
00:12:43.455 --> 00:12:45.510
you don't actually
have to generate

244
00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:48.555
the words one at a
time during training.

245
00:12:48.555 --> 00:12:50.415
Instead, what masking does

246
00:12:50.415 --> 00:12:52.680
is it blocks out
the last part of

247
00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:55.545
the sentence to mimic what

248
00:12:55.545 --> 00:12:57.300
the network will need to do

249
00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.340
at test time or
during prediction.

250
00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:00.885
In other words, all that

251
00:13:00.885 --> 00:13:04.170
mask multi -head
attention does is it

252
00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:06.300
repeatedly pretends

253
00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:10.155
that the network had
perfectly translated,

254
00:13:10.155 --> 00:13:14.370
say the first few
words and hides

255
00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:17.370
the remaining words
to see if given

256
00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:21.735
a perfect first part
of the translation,

257
00:13:21.735 --> 00:13:24.525
whether the new
network can predict

258
00:13:24.525 --> 00:13:26.940
the next word in the
sequence accurately.

259
00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:29.655
That's a summary of the
transform architecture.

260
00:13:29.655 --> 00:13:33.915
Since the paper attention
is all you need came out,

261
00:13:33.915 --> 00:13:35.220
there have been many other

